# tried X → failed because Y → did Z

2026-10-20 stage1: tried spoofing a Chrome User-Agent on plain HTTP requests → Wikipedia returned 403 ("respect our robot policy"): a Chrome UA from a non-browser TLS client is itself a bot signal, and Wikimedia wants a UA with contact info → plain HTTP now sends an honest "BriefAgent/0.1 (...; +URL)" UA; only the real headless Chrome uses a Chrome UA.
2026-10-20 stage1: tried stopping the fallback chain only on final statuses from plain HTTP → the browser saw a real 404 but the reader service then returned Wikipedia's "page does not exist" text as a success → any method's not_found/unsafe/too_large now ends the chain, and the reader's "Target URL returned error 404" warning is mapped to not_found.
2026-10-20 stage1: tried httpbin.org/delay/30 as the timeout test → httpbin caps delays at 10 s and returned JSON (unsupported) → use a blackholed port (example.com:81) to test timeouts.
2026-10-20 stage1: tried parsing the archive.org availability API as JSON directly → it rate-limits (HTTP 429) and returns an HTML error page → check the status code first; 429 becomes "blocked: archive.org rate-limited".
2026-10-20 stage1: reader service timed out at 15 s although its limit was 25 s → the httpx client's own read timeout (15 s) applied first → pass each method's time limit to the request itself.
2026-10-20 stage1→2: tried a 500-char "empty" threshold in stage 1 as the final verdict → a very short but valid article would end as fetch: empty and never reach stage 2 → stage 1 uses the threshold only to decide on fallbacks; short-but-clean pages are accepted as ok (short), and stage 2 decides with a ~150-char threshold plus the LLM page-type check.
2026-10-20 stage2: tried trafilatura's default date extraction → it guessed dates from years mentioned in the text ("2024-01-01" for an undated page) → turned off its text-based date search; dates now carry a date_confidence and the brief treats them as hints.
2026-10-20 stage2: tried keeping a reference list as one footnote block → Wikipedia's 33k-char reference list scored highest and used most of the 40k budget (article coverage 38%) → one block per reference, and footnotes only fill leftover budget.
2026-10-20 stage2: tried selecting only paragraphs that contain topic words → "AI / job / losses" missed paragraphs saying "unemployment" or "automation" and left budget unused → leftover budget goes to blocks next to the selected ones (stage 0 synonyms will improve scoring); Wikipedia coverage 25% → 57%.
2026-10-20 stage2: tried adding context blocks before tables → neighbouring paragraphs used up the budget and tables beside selected text were dropped → tables (numeric evidence) are added before the context fill.
2026-10-20 stage0: tried gemini-pro-latest as the only planner model → this API key has 0 Pro quota (429, "limit: 0") and the run fell back to the generic plan → models are tried in order (Pro → Flash → Flash-Lite); quota errors with limit 0 and 404s skip straight to the next model; the plan records which model wrote it.
2026-10-20 stage0: tried caching every plan → a fallback plan caused by a temporary quota error was saved and would have been reused on the next run → fallback plans are never reused from the cache.
2026-10-20 stage0: tried retrying timeouts on the same model → gemini-flash-latest timed out twice (90 s each), so planning took 3 minutes → a timeout moves straight to the next model; the per-call limit is 60 s.
2026-10-20 stage0→2: tried exact matching for short keywords → "job" never matched "jobs", so a paragraph on automation and jobs scored as unrelated → short words also match their plurals.
2026-10-20 stage0→2: tried ranking paragraphs by raw keyword hits → long, loosely related history paragraphs beat short on-topic ones once the plan's synonyms were added → rank by relevance per character (damped for paragraphs over 300 chars).
2026-10-20 stage3: tried only checking that the number appears in the quote → "half of all white-collar workers" (= 50%) was dropped as number_not_in_quote → fraction words (half, a third, one in five…) and "x5" multipliers count as numbers.
2026-10-20 stage3: tried treating "N% of …" as proof of a level reading → "AI may cut up to 15% of clerical roles", recorded as a 15% relative cut (also correct), was dropped as number_misread → change verbs before "N% of" allow the relative reading, and a mismatch against this weak pattern downgrades to unknown instead of dropping.
2026-10-20 stage3: tried merging near-identical statements within a source → "Finding 1 … wave 1" and "Finding 2 … wave 2" were merged as repeats → statements that differ in any number are never merged.
2026-10-20 stage3: tried leaving metric choice to the extraction model plus the LLM support check → both tagged "41% of bosses" / "49% of firms" as a share of workers losing jobs → code compares what the number counts (of_what) with what the metric counts (workers vs organisations).
2026-10-20 stage3: tried capping claims with numbers first, then round-robin across questions → 4 country figures from one Oxford/Citi study pushed out the OECD's contrary "only 9% at risk" → within a question, one claim per origin before a second from any origin.
2026-10-20 stage3: tried letting the support check change modality whenever it disagreed → it turned "There were around 100 employees" into an opinion → modality changes need an explicit modality flag. Spot check: 17/19 checker verdicts correct (docs/reviews/stage3-support-spotcheck.md).
2026-10-20 stage4-5 (planning): tried one combined "weakest" scope relation per pair → it needed an arbitrary ordering between dimensions, lost why claims differ, and treated nested scopes (US ⊂ global) like partial overlaps → scope relations per dimension; contradictions need `same` on every dimension; nested = part vs whole (complementary).
2026-10-20 stage4-5: tried retrying unavailable models on every call → an overloaded Flash cost 60 s per call, and Pro's zero quota was hit again on every call → the model chain remembers unavailable models for the run (quota/404 for the whole run, timeouts for 5 minutes).
2026-10-20 stage4: tried applying geography aliases only to the source's wording → the LLM's own label "European Union" didn't match the built-in EU ⊂ global relation → aliases are also applied to LLM labels and hierarchy entries.
2026-10-20 stage5: tried listing scope differences pair by pair → an 11-claim cluster produced dozens of "China vs Thailand" lines → one line per dimension ("varies by geography: China, US, …").
2026-10-20 demo run: tried trafilatura on a browser-rendered WEF page → it extracted the Cookiebot consent table as the article, stage 1 said ok and stage 2 rated it highly relevant from the title → prune consent-manager elements (Cookiebot/OneTrust/Didomi/…) before extraction, a `consent_wall` status when the text is mostly cookie language, and relevance judged on body text, not title. WEF went from 0 claims to 10.
2026-10-20 demo run: tried accepting fraction words as numbers → "two-thirds" was stored as 0.67 percent → a decimal ≤1 with a percent unit that matches a fraction word ×100 is rescaled.
2026-10-20 demo run: tried clustering only claims with "the same kind of measure" → IMF "40% of employment exposed" and Goldman "two-thirds of occupations exposed" were never compared → cluster by sub-question; stage 5 decides comparability. Now one cluster: 10 origins across 3 sources.
2026-10-20 demo run: tried showing the judge LLM's one-line summary → it said "sources disagree" on a cluster the rules labelled complementary → the brief headlines code-written wording; the LLM sentence is kept as llm_summary for inspection only.
2026-10-20 demo run: tried treating a named claim and an unattributed claim from the same source as two origins → one WEF press release produced a fake "consensus" → claims from one source are independent only if both cite different origins explicitly.
2026-10-20 full run: tried matching origin names by spelling only → "CBO" and "Congressional Budget Office" counted as different origins, so one report looked like agreement → acronyms match the initials of multi-word names ("imf staff" ↔ "international monetary fund").
2026-10-20 full run: tried clustering within each research question → extraction tagged CBO's projection, EPI's review and the Wisconsin study to different questions, so they were never compared → runs with ≤60 claims are clustered in one call across questions.
2026-10-20 full run: tried showing out-of-plan clusters as a 2-claim teaser → the Wisconsin "no job loss" evidence was hidden → multi-origin clusters outside the plan are rendered as normal findings, marked as outside the questions.
2026-10-20 spot check: tried letting overlapping numbers decide agreement when no plan metric applied → "41% of jobs" (Taub) and "a quarter to half of the workload" (Goldman) became a consensus → numbers must count the same thing (of_what), even under the same metric; otherwise the pair is different_measures.
2026-10-20 spot check: tried accepting the judge LLM's "agree" on its own → a catch-all cluster ($7.25 minimum wage, Wisconsin earnings, inflation erosion) became a consensus because nothing conflicted → LLM-only agreement needs the statements to share a subject (wording overlap), otherwise "unrelated". All 3 consensus verdicts across both demo runs were false positives; 0 remain.
2026-10-20 brief: tried giving the bottom-line writer internal origin labels → it wrote "Source s3 notes…" → the writer gets publisher names, and the checker rejects internal IDs.
2026-10-20 run without a key: the README told users to `cp .env.example .env.local`, which silently overwrote an existing .env.local (and its API key) → every LLM stage fell back quietly, extraction failed for 5/5 sources, and the run "succeeded" with an empty brief plus SDK shutdown tracebacks → `cp -n` in the README; `run` and `extract` stop at the start when no key is found; the summary writer is skipped without a key (no half-built SDK client) and goes straight to the template after an error; stage lines say "extraction failed for N/M sources (reason)", and the run stops if every source failed.
2026-10-20 adk web: tried publishing brief.html as a text/html ADK artifact for viewing in the dev UI → the dev UI deliberately rewrites text/html (and SVG/XML) artifacts to application/octet-stream before opening them (XSS protection), so clicking downloads instead of rendering; it also hid the text of an event that carried an artifact → the log message and the artifact are two events, and the message explains to download/open brief.html (the file path is shown too).
2026-10-20 adk web: tried taking the topic as "the message minus the URLs" → a natural request ('i need to create a brief on "How AI will change the job market" based on sources: …') became the whole sentence → a quoted phrase is the topic; otherwise request wording and "based on these sources:" tails are stripped.
2026-10-20 adk web: tried a full run log in the chat (started + finished messages with every detail) → too noisy to follow → the chat shows one line per stage, the fetched/usable link counts with any dropped links and why, stop reasons, and a "Brief ready" link; details go to the terminal log, and silent stages post no event (state-only events showed as empty rows).
2026-10-20 adk web: tried the HTML artifact as the way to open the brief → ADK's dev UI always hands HTML artifacts to the browser as a download → a localhost-only viewer (serves runs/*/brief.html and nothing else) and a "Brief ready" link with target="_blank", which the dev UI's sanitiser allows.
2026-10-20 refactor: found while splitting stage 3's per-source function: every source appended dropped claims to one shared list and counted its own as the tail of that list since it started; sources run in parallel, so a source's `dropped_by_reason` could include another source's drops (a regression test reproduced s1 reporting 3 drops instead of 1) → each source keeps its own drop list, merged at the end.
2026-10-20 adk web: tried passing the summary writer's (LlmAgent) events through to the chat → its raw JSON reply appeared as a message, and its bullets carried internal IDs like "[q2-k2]" into the brief → the writer runs silently and hands its draft to the checker via a per-run handoff object (no chat event, no state-only row); the checker strips bracketed finding IDs from bullet text, and the prompt says IDs belong only in finding_ids.
